Project Requirement: Build an Unused Code Detection, Reporting & Auto-Fix Feature

I have an existing project in ZIP format. I will upload the ZIP file. Your task is to carefully analyze the entire existing project and implement a new feature called Unused Code Detection, Detailed Reporting, and Automatic Cleanup.

1. Important Instructions
First, extract and understand the complete existing project structure.
Analyze the existing architecture, programming languages, frameworks, UI, and functionality.
Do not unnecessarily modify existing code.
Do not change the existing UI, design, layout, colors, styling, or functionality.
Keep all existing features working exactly as they currently work.
Add the new feature by following the existing project's architecture and coding standards.
Do not remove or overwrite any existing functionality.
Make sure the implementation works with the actual project, not just a demonstration or hardcoded example.
2. Unused CSS Detection

The system should scan all CSS files in the uploaded project and identify:

CSS classes that are used in the project.
CSS classes that are not used anywhere in the project.
CSS classes that are used in only one page.
CSS classes that are used in multiple pages.
CSS selectors that are partially used or potentially unused.
CSS rules that are duplicated or overridden, where safely identifiable.
Example

Suppose a CSS file contains:

css
.cl-wd-100 {
    width: 100%;
}

.background-green {
    background: green;
}

.margin-50 {
    margin: 50px;
}

.font-size-95 {
    font-size: 95px;
}

.col-wd-50 {
    width: 50%;
}

.col-tab-25 {
    width: 25%;
}

.col-mob-100 {
    width: 100%;
}

.background-red {
    background: red;
}


These classes are used in different pages.

Page 1:

HTML
<div class="cl-wd-100 background-green margin-50">
    <h1 class="font-size-95">Welcome testing text</h1>
</div>


Page 2:

HTML
<div class="col-wd-50 col-tab-25 col-mob-100 background-red">
    <h1>Hello World my stl css</h1>
</div>


Expected result:

All classes used in Page 1 should be identified as used.
All classes used in Page 2 should be identified as used.
Classes used only in Page 1 should be reported as used only in Page 1.
Classes used only in Page 2 should be reported as used only in Page 2.
Any CSS classes not used anywhere in the entire project should be reported as unused.

Important: Do not consider a CSS class unused just because it is not present in every page. Search the entire project before marking it unused.

