4. Code Highlighting and Color Identification

When displaying the report:

Used CSS code must appear in black, grey, or the existing neutral code-editor color.
Unused CSS code must be highlighted in RED.
Potentially unused code must have a separate warning indicator.
File paths and line numbers must be clearly visible.
Highlight the complete unused CSS rule, not just its class name.

Example:

css
/* USED - Normal grey/black text */
.background-green {
    background: green;
}

/* UNUSED - Highlight this complete rule in RED */
.unused-class {
    color: blue;
    padding: 20px;
}


The actual UI should use syntax highlighting rather than changing the original source code colors.

5. Apply Fix Functionality

Add an Apply Fix option to the unused code report.

When the user clicks Apply Fix:

Remove only the confirmed unused CSS rules.
Preserve all used CSS rules.
Preserve the original order of remaining CSS rules.
Preserve comments wherever possible.
Preserve media queries.
Preserve responsive styles.
Preserve CSS variables and dependencies.
Do not modify unrelated code.
Update the CSS file with the cleaned content.
Refresh the report after applying the fix.
Important safety requirement
Show a confirmation message before modifying files.
Create a backup or provide an undo option.
Do not automatically delete potentially unused CSS.
Do not remove CSS referenced dynamically through JavaScript, PHP, templates, generated HTML, or runtime class names.
Do not delete CSS used by third-party integrations or external components unless usage can be established.
If usage cannot be determined confidently, mark it as Potentially Unused instead of deleting it.
